---
// The five lesson blocks. They are §1–§5 of public/js/cbf.js, in the same order.
// Everything inside .x is what Expert mode reveals, in place.
---
<section class="lesson">
  <h2>How it works</h2>

  <!-- 1 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>1</i>Where the end effector is</h3>
    <span class="eq">p(q) = ( L₁ cos q₁ + L₂ cos(q₁+q₂) ,  L₁ sin q₁ + L₂ sin(q₁+q₂) )</span>
    <p>Two angles decide where the end effector is, and everything below is a rule about that one
    point.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <span class="eq">ṗ = J(q) q̇ ,   J(q) = [ −L₁s₁ − L₂s₁₂ ,  −L₂s₁₂ ;  L₁c₁ + L₂c₁₂ ,  L₂c₁₂ ] <span class="c">det J = L₁L₂ sin q₂</span></span>
      <p>The Jacobian turns joint speeds into end-effector speed. Its first column is
      ẑ × (end effector − base) and its second is ẑ × (end effector − elbow): each column is the
      velocity you get by spinning the segment it belongs to. det J vanishes at q₂ = 0 or ±π — arm fully stretched or fully folded — and there
      the radial direction is unreachable at any joint speed. The command is the joint velocity
      itself, q̇ = u, which is what makes everything below linear in u.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 2 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>2</i>Where you want to go</h3>
    <span class="eq">v = (p* − p) · <span class="up">min</span>( k<sub>p</sub> ,  v<sub>max</sub> / ‖p* − p‖ ) <span class="c">then</span> (J<sup>T</sup>J + λ²I) u<sub>nom</sub> = J<sup>T</sup>v</span>
    <p>Head straight at the target, never faster than the speed cap. This controller has never heard
    of the obstacle and it is never replanned — every curve you are about to see is the filter’s
    doing, not a smarter plan.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <p>The cap is applied to the Cartesian velocity <em>before</em> the inverse, so the unfiltered
      motion is a genuine constant-speed straight line — the reference the detour is read against.
      The inverse is damped least squares rather than J⁻¹, and det(JᵀJ + λ²I) ≥ λ⁴ &gt; 0 for every
      pose, so there is no singular case to special-case; writing <code>if (|det J| &lt; ε)</code>
      would put back exactly the discontinuity that damping exists to remove. λ is a length and
      scales with the arm (0.10 m here), which caps the joint-speed gain, so near full stretch the
      arm refuses the radial command and sweeps sideways instead of exploding. Anything genuinely
      time-optimal would need a double-integrator model and a higher-order CBF — a different lesson.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 3 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>3</i>What is forbidden</h3>
    <span class="eq">h(p) = √(ab) · [ ( (|x′|/a)<sup>2/ε</sup> + (|y′|/b)<sup>2/ε</sup> )<sup>ε/2</sup> − 1 ]</span>
    <table class="h">
      <tr><td>h &gt; 0</td><td>outside — safe</td></tr>
      <tr><td>h = 0</td><td>exactly on the boundary</td></tr>
      <tr><td>h &lt; 0</td><td>inside — the rule is violated</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>One formula draws every forbidden region: ε = 1 is the ellipse you drag out yourself, and a
    small ε squares off the corners so the region hugs the rectangular obstacle. The obstacle and a
    region you drew are the same rule, one more h. The √(ab) in front turns the shape value into metres
    of clearance, so h is very nearly the gap you can see on screen.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <span class="eq">∂h/∂x = √(ab) · ( (|x′|/a) / F )<sup>2/ε − 1</sup> · <span class="up">sgn</span>(x′) / a <span class="c">F is the bracket above</span></span>
      <p>Taking the ε/2-th root of the superquadric sum is a monotone rescale that fixes zero, so the
      zero level set, the safe set and the CBF property are all untouched — but the raw sum grows
      like distance<sup>2/ε</sup>, which at ε = 0.25 overflows outright far from the
      shape and would throttle the arm to a fraction of a sensible approach speed while it is still
      half a metre away. With the root, ‖∇h‖ stays between √(b/a) and √(a/b), so h is the signed
      distance to within that factor and is exact for a circle — which is what lets one α mean the
      same thing for both shapes. The absolute values cause no kink for ε &lt; 2, and ∇h vanishes at
      exactly one point, the centre, which the code guards. There is no separate clearance term: the
      outline you see <em>is</em> the h = 0 curve, and the region is the whole statement of what is
      forbidden.</p>
      <p>The obstacle is a rectangle and stays one; ε shapes the region <em>around</em> it, which is
      always the superellipse that just contains the rectangle — at the corner (a, b) we need
      (a/A)<sup>2/ε</sup> + (b/B)<sup>2/ε</sup> = 1, so A = 2<sup>ε/2</sup>·a. At ε → 0 the region is
      the rectangle itself; at ε = 1 it is the ellipse circumscribing it. Slide ε and watch the same
      object acquire a tighter or a more generous rule.</p>
      <p>On vocabulary: the set where h ≥ 0 is the <em>safe set</em>, and that name is standard. Its
      complement has no single canonical name — papers say unsafe set or avoid set, applications say
      obstacle, and aerospace work says keep-out zone. “Forbidden region” is the phrasing used here,
      and it is also the established term in the virtual-fixtures literature, where a
      forbidden-region virtual fixture is exactly this: a software constraint that keeps a tool out
      of a volume.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>4</i>How h is allowed to change</h3>
    <span class="eq">ḣ = ∂h/∂p · ∂p/∂q · q̇ = <span class="up">∇</span>h(p)<sup>T</sup> J(q) u  ≥  −α h</span>
    <p>The rule is not “never get closer than a metre”. It is: <em>the closer you are, the slower you
    may approach.</em> −αh is an approach budget that uses itself up — on the boundary h = 0, so the
    budget is zero, so ḣ ≥ 0, and you are guided <em>along</em> the boundary instead of through it.
    Because h is in metres, α reads off directly: at α = 2 you may close at 2 m/s for every metre of
    clearance, so at 10 cm you may close at 20 cm/s. In Expert mode that number is the ruler drawn
    on the end effector; watch it shrink to nothing as the arm arrives.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <span class="eq">h(t) ≥ h(0) e<sup>−αt</sup></span>
      <p>α·h is the simplest extended class-K function; any strictly increasing α(·) with α(0) = 0
      works, and it must be defined for h &lt; 0 too, which is what makes the line above legal there.
      Enforcing the inequality for all time gives that bound by the comparison lemma: forward
      invariance if you start safe, and exponential recovery if you start inside a region. Because
      the robot is velocity-commanded, u already appears in the <em>first</em> derivative of h
      (relative degree 1), which is why a single inequality that is linear in u suffices — command
      torque instead and u drops out of ḣ, and you need a higher-order CBF. The guarantee is
      continuous-time; we integrate with explicit Euler at Δt = 1/240 s, and αΔt ≤ 1 is what carries
      the argument across the discretisation (at the maximum α on the slider, αΔt ≈ 0.08).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>5</i>The filter</h3>
    <span class="eq">u = <span class="up">arg min</span> ‖u − u<sub>nom</sub>‖²  <span class="c">subject to</span>  <span class="up">∇</span>h<sup>T</sup> J u ≥ −α h <span class="c">for every region</span></span>
    <p>If the command is already safe, the filter hands it back untouched — that is what it means
    when the ghost arrow is nowhere to be seen, sitting exactly under the solid one. Otherwise it
    makes the smallest change that obeys every rule at once.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <p>u lives in ℝ² and there are at most two rows, so the answer is one of four points: u<sub>nom</sub> itself, its projection onto either row alone, or the intersection of
      both. Try all four and keep the feasible one of least cost — that is exact, because the
      objective is strictly convex, so the solution is the unique projection onto the feasible
      polygon, and no multiplier sign test is needed.</p>
      <p>The filter can never fail while you are safe: if every h ≥ 0 then every right-hand side
      −αh ≤ 0, so u = 0 satisfies every row. Infeasibility requires some h to be negative already,
      and when it happens the enumeration certifies it for free — we stop the arm and say so rather
      than adding a slack variable and quietly trading away the guarantee.</p>
      <p>Two honest caveats. “Minimal” is minimal in <em>joint</em> space; in task space the
      correction direction is JJᵀ∇h rather than ∇h, so the end effector also slows as it slides along
      a boundary, not only turns — that is correct, not a glitch. And safety is not liveness: the arm
      can legitimately stall with the target behind a region, and nothing in this maths promises
      otherwise. A filter keeps you safe; a planner makes you arrive.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <blockquote>
    <b>WHAT is forbidden lives in h. HOW CAUTIOUSLY you approach lives in α.</b>
    <span>Draw a new shape and you have changed h. Move one slider and you have changed α.
    Nothing else in the controller needs to know.</span>
  </blockquote>
</section>
